    {
      "source": "Doctors of Running",
      "priority": 3,
      "url": "https://www.doctorsofrunning.com/2025/01/nike-zoom-fly-6-review-2024-release.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The ride is softer and more cushioned than aggressive, with good impact protection from the high stack and a soft, well-beveled heel; the forefoot feels firmer/stiffer at first due to the plate and rocker until break-in.",
        "Responsiveness: ZoomX plus the carbon plate gives some energy return, but the shoe is described as more soft than responsive overall; toe-off becomes slightly stiff but snappy after break-in and is better for tempo/long runs than max-speed efforts.",
        "Stability: Forefoot stiffness from the plate/rocker adds torsional control, and the snug midfoot lockdown with a moderately stiff heel counter improves security; some runners may notice initial lateral heel-collar pressure.",
        "Durability: Overall durability is expected to be average to slightly above average, with a more robust/training-friendly upper and good forefoot outsole grip, but the heel outsole shows earlier wear than the forefoot."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Road Trail Run",
      "priority": 4,
      "url": "https://www.roadtrailrun.com/2024/11/nike-zoomfly-6-multi-tester-review-5.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: Zoom Fly 6 is much softer and plusher than Zoom Fly 5, with a 42/34 mm stack that feels highly protective (especially in the forefoot) without turning mushy.",
        "Responsiveness: The ZoomX + firmer SRO2 foam and full-length carbon Flyplate create controlled bounce, snappier toe-off, and strong perceived energy return.",
        "Stability: Lateral support is generally good for a high-stack plated shoe with secure foot hold, but heel strikers may notice some rearfoot instability from the cantilevered heel geometry.",
        "Durability: Outsole coverage is substantial (waffle forefoot rubber plus large heel rubber sections) with solid wet-road grip, though long-term wear data is limited in the provided text."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Believe in the Run",
      "priority": 5,
      "url": "https://believeintherun.com/shoe-reviews/nike-zoom-fly-6-review/",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The dual-layer midsole feels soft on top (ZoomX) and firmer/supportive underneath (SR-02), giving high-stack impact protection with a firm-resilient ride rather than a plush one.",
        "Responsiveness: The carbon Flyplate and rocker geometry deliver a propulsive, smooth turnover that handles faster efforts well, though it has less pop/bounce than the Alphafly.",
        "Stability: Despite the tall stack, reviewers reported stable cornering and confidence at speed, helped by the firmer SR-02 base, secure upper lockdown, and strong wet-surface traction.",
        "Durability: Early signs were positive (rubber waffle outsole grip and no immediate material issues), but the review did not provide long-term outsole wear or upper longevity results."
      ]
    }
